      "",
      "*Choose the correct option in each case.* 1.The level of formality a speaker chooses is called… A. dialect B. register C. accent D. jargon 2.Which of these is a feature of formal language? A. slang B. contracted forms C. complete sentences D. nicknames",
      "",
      "3.A question that cannot be answered with yes or no is said to be… A. closed B. leading C. open D. rhetorical 4.The special vocabulary of a trade or profession is called… A. slang B. jargon C. register D. idiom 5.*Don't* is an example of a… A. contracted form B. landmark C. non-verbal cue D. tone 6.Varying the pace, volume, tone and stress of the voice is called… A. articulation B. elaboration C. voice modulation D. intonation",
      "",
      "7.Which of these is a landmark you could use when giving directions? A. about ten minutes B. turn left C. the filling station D. go straight",
      "",
      "8.Which sentence is in the correct register for a job interview? A. Gimme the form. B. I wanna apply. C. I would like to apply for the position. D. You get the form or not?",
      "",
      "9.In *a REcord* and *to reCORD*, the change of meaning is carried by the… A. spelling B. stress C. volume D. pace",
      "",
      "10. A nod or a smile that shows you are listening is called a… A. contracted form B. non-verbal cue C. closed question D. landmark",
      "",
      "#### Section B — Theory and written response",
      "",
      "*Answer all four questions.* 1.(a) Explain what is meant by register and state four things that decide which register a speaker should use. (b) List four features of formal language and four features of informal language. (c) Rewrite each of the following in formal English: Gimme that book; Wanna come?; He can't do it; That guy's talking rubbish. (d) Rewrite the following so that it is suitable for a close friend: *I would be most grateful if you would accompany me to the ceremony.*",
      "",
      "2.(a) Explain the difference between an open and a closed question, giving one example of each. (b) Turn each of these closed questions into an open question: Did you like the book?; Is child labour wrong?; Was the journey long? (c) Write a conversation of eight turns between two learners about a festival, in which one speaker uses at least three open questions to draw out the other. (d) State two things a speaker should add to a bare answer of *yes* or *no*, and explain why.",
      "",
      "3.(a) Name three things that make a spoken description hold a listener's attention. (b) Rewrite the following description so that it is more exact and better varied: *I went to the market. It was nice. There were many people. I bought some things and came home.* (c) Describe, in a paragraph of about eight sentences, an experience of your own at a hospital, a lorry station or a festival. (d) State one thing you must be careful to do when you describe an experience that happened to somebody else rather than to you.",
      "",
      "4.(a) A stranger at your school gate asks the way to the nearest clinic. Write out your directions in full, in the correct order, using at least three landmarks, one expression of distance and one expression of position. (b)",
