      "",
      "Explain why *it's over there near the school* is not an acceptable direction. (c) Name the four features of the voice that a speaker can vary and state what each is for. (d) Write the sentence *I did not say she took the money* three times, underlining a different word each time, and give the meaning each stress produces.",
      "",
      "#### Section C — Listening and speaking",
      "",
      "*These tasks are carried out aloud and assessed as you perform them.* 1.Role-play, with a partner and before the class, one formal and one informal version of the same errand — for example asking for a form at an office and asking a friend for the same form. Your listeners should be able to say, without being told, which was which, and to name two features that told them.",
      "",
      "2.Interview a partner for three minutes about a subject they know well, using at least four open questions. Your partner should speak for at least three times as long as you do. Afterwards, state which of your questions produced the fullest answer, and why.",
      "",
      "3.Read a paragraph of about a hundred words aloud to the class, using pace, volume, tone and stress deliberately, and maintaining appropriate eye contact. Before you read, mark on your copy the three words you intend to stress, and afterwards explain to the class why you chose them.",
      "",
      "#### Section D — Portfolio and project",
      "",
      "*These pieces are collected in your portfolio over a longer period.* 1.Keep a register journal for one week. Record at least ten conversations, noting for each the listener, the setting, the purpose and the register you used. Mark any in which you now think you chose wrongly, and write a short reflection of one paragraph on what you have noticed about your own speech.",
