      ">",
      "> A health officer visits your school and speaks for ten minutes about preventing malaria. (a) State the purpose you would expect the talk to have, and name two phrases that would confirm it. (b) Write the main idea you would expect, in one sentence. (c) Set out the shape of the notes you would take, showing how you would number the points and where you would keep figures. (d) Write four questions you would ask at the end, at",
      "",
      "",
      "",
      "> least two of them about evidence. (e) Write an opinion of the talk in three parts — what you think, your reason, and the evidence — assuming the officer gave figures for cases but none for costs. (f) Explain how you would cross-check your notes before writing them up, and say what kind of error that step is most likely to catch.",
