      "#### Engage",
      "",
      "Two learners read the same page. One reaches the bottom and can tell you what happened, what the writer was trying to do, which sentence gave it away, and what she thinks of it. The other reaches the bottom and has to go back to the top. Both have moved their eyes across the same words. The difference is not speed and it is not cleverness — it is that the first one was doing several things at once while she read, and every one of them can be learnt.",
      "",
      "#### 4.1 Reading with pictures in the mind",
      "",
      "Good readers see what they read. As the words go in, a picture builds: the place, the people, the weather, the noise. This is called **visualisation**, and it is the single habit that most separates a reader who remembers a text from one who does not.",
      "",
      "Two other habits go with it. **Monitoring** means keeping a small part of your attention on the question *am I still following this?* **Self-correction** is what you do when the answer is no: you stop, go back to the last place that made sense, and read forward again more slowly. Weak readers plough on; strong readers go back.",
      "",
      "> **Passage 4.1 — The last day of the harmattan**",
      ">",
      "> *A narrative text. Read it once for the story, then again slowly, building the picture.*",
      ">",
      "> The dust had been in everything for nine weeks. It lay on the leaves of the mango tree so thickly that Adzo could write her name on them with one finger, and by evening the wind would have rubbed it out again. It was in the water, in the rice, in the folds of her school uniform. At night she slept with a cloth over her face.",
      ">",
      "> Her grandmother had said, on the first morning of December, that the harmattan would break on a Thursday.",
      ">",
      "> She had not said which Thursday. Adzo had asked her twice and both times the old woman had only looked out at the white sky and said, *You will know.*",
      ">",
      "> On the Thursday it broke, Adzo was carrying a bucket from the standpipe. She was thinking about nothing at all. Then she stopped, and the bucket knocked against her leg and soaked her foot, and she did not notice, because the air had changed. It was not cooler. It was heavier, and it smelt of something she had forgotten — wet earth, from somewhere far off, coming ahead of the rain like a message sent in advance.",
      "",
