      "#### Engage",
      "",
      "*The bus.* That is not a sentence. *Left at six.* That is not one either. Put them together — *The bus left at six* — and suddenly there is something a listener can agree with, doubt or act on. Every sentence in English, however long, is built from those two parts. Learn to see them and you can take any sentence apart, however tangled, and put together sentences of your own that do not fall over.",
      "",
      "#### 7.1 Subject and predicate",
      "",
      "Every sentence has two parts. The **subject** names who or what the sentence is about. The **predicate** says something about that subject. Neither can be left out.",
      "",
      "| **Subject** | **Predicate** |",
      "| --- | --- |",
      "| The bus | left at six. |",
      "| Adzo's grandmother | had been watching the hills every Thursday. |",
      "| Mining along the river banks | has changed the colour of the water. |",
      "| She | sings. |",
      "| The eleven weavers in the Bonwire workshop | produce cloth for buyers in five countries. |",
      "",
      "*Table 7.1 — Every sentence divides in two.*",
      "",
      "##### 7.1.1 Finding the subject",
      "",
      "To find the subject, first find the verb, then ask **who or what** is doing it. The answer is the subject. Two distinctions help.",
      "",
      "The **simple subject** is the head noun or pronoun by itself: *grandmother*, *mining*, *she*, *weavers*.",
      "",
      "- The **complete subject** is that head noun with everything attached to it: *Adzo's grandmother*, *mining*",
      "",
      "- *along the river banks*, *the eleven weavers in the Bonwire workshop*.",
      "",
      "> **Common misconception 7.1**",
      ">",
      "> The subject is not always the first word, and it is not always short. In *In November 2016 the road was finished*, the subject is *the road*, not *November*. Find the verb first — *was finished* — then ask what was finished.",
      "",
      "##### 7.1.2 What the predicate contains",
      "",
      "The predicate always contains the verb, and may contain a good deal besides: an object, a complement, and any adverbs or phrases that go with the verb.",
      "",
      "| **Sentence** | **Verb** | **Object or complement** | **Extra** |",
      "| --- | --- | --- | --- |",
      "| The class won the game. | won | the game | — |",
      "| Adzo was very tired. | was | very tired | — |",
      "| She gave her sister the letter yesterday. | gave | her sister, the letter | yesterday |",
      "| The road runs through the hills. | runs | — | through the hills |",
      "",
      "*Table 7.2 — Inside the predicate.*",
      "",
      "> **Practice 7.1**",
