      ">",
      "> 3. Give the simple subject and the complete subject of: *The eleven weavers in the Bonwire workshop produce cloth for five countries.*",
      ">",
      "> 4. Explain the method for finding the subject of a long sentence.",
      ">",
      "> 5. Write four sentences and mark the subject and predicate of each.",
      "",
      "#### 7.2 Clauses",
      "",
      "A **clause** is a group of words containing a subject and a verb. A **phrase** does not have both. That single difference decides a great deal.",
      "",
      "| **Group of words** | **Subject?** | **Verb?** | **Clause or phrase?** |",
      "| --- | --- | --- | --- |",
      "| We danced at the party all night long | we | danced | clause |",
      "| at the party all night long | no | no | phrase |",
      "| because the bus broke down | the bus | broke down | clause |",
      "| in front of the house | no | no | phrase |",
      "| although she was tired | she | was | clause |",
      "",
      "*Table 7.3 — Clause or phrase?*",
      "",
      "##### 7.2.1 Independent and dependent clauses",
      "",
      "An **independent clause** makes complete sense on its own; it could be written as a sentence and nobody would ask what was missing. A **dependent clause** — also called a **subordinate clause** — has a subject and a verb but leaves you waiting.",
      "",
      "| **Clause** | **Type** | **Why** |",
      "| --- | --- | --- |",
      "| We danced at the party all night long | independent | it stands alone as a sentence |",
      "| Kofi arrived late | independent | it stands alone |",
      "| because the bus broke down | dependent | because leaves you waiting for the rest |",
      "| although she was tired | dependent | although leaves you waiting |",
      "| when we came | dependent | when leaves you waiting |",
      "| they were at a meeting | independent | it stands alone |",
      "",
      "*Table 7.4 — Independent against dependent.*",
      "",
      "What makes a clause dependent is usually the word at the front of it: a subordinating conjunction such as *because*, *although*, *when*, *since*, *while*, *if*, *until*, *whereas*, or a relative pronoun such as *who*, *which* or *that*. Take that word away and the clause stands up again: *the bus broke down* is a sentence; *because the bus broke down* is not.",
      "",
      "> **Common misconception 7.2**",
      ">",
      "> A dependent clause punctuated as a sentence is a **fragment**, and it is one of the most heavily penalised errors in written English. *Because the bus broke down.* is not a sentence, however long it looks. Attach it: *We were late because the bus broke down.*",
      "",
      "",
      "",
      "> **Practice 7.2**",
