      "> 1. Explain the difference between a clause and a phrase, with an example of each.",
      ">",
      "> 2. State whether each is a clause or a phrase: *at the party all night long; because the bus broke down; in front of the house; although she was tired.*",
      ">",
      "> 3. State whether each clause is independent or dependent: *We danced all night; when we came; they were at a meeting; since it was raining.*",
      ">",
      "> 4. Name six words that make a clause dependent.",
      ">",
      "> 5. Correct these fragments by attaching them to an independent clause: *Because the bus broke down.* · *Although she had studied hard.*",
      "",
      "#### 7.3 Simple and compound sentences",
      "",
      "A **simple sentence** is one independent clause. It may be long, but it has only one subject-and-verb backbone.",
      "",
      "A **compound sentence** joins two or more independent clauses. Each half could have stood alone; they are joined because the ideas belong together.",
      "",
      "| **Way of joining** | **Example** | **Note** |",
      "| --- | --- | --- |",
      "| comma + coordinating conjunction | Kofi arrived late, **and** he missed the examination. | the commonest method; the comma goes before the conjunction |",
      "| semicolon | Kofi arrived late; he missed the examination. | use when the link is obvious without a word for it |",
      "| semicolon + linking adverb | Kofi arrived late; **consequently**, he missed the examination. | the adverb is followed by a comma |",
      "",
      "*Table 7.5 — Three ways of forming a compound sentence.*",
      "",
      "> **Model 7.1 — Building and taking apart a compound sentence**",
      ">",
      "> **Two independent clauses:**",
      ">",
      "> 1. Kofi arrived late.",
      ">",
      "> 2. He missed the examination.",
      ">",
      "> **Joined with a coordinating conjunction:**",
