      "> Clause 1: *The road was finished in 2016* ✔ stands alone",
      ">",
      "> Clause 2: *buyers now come to the farm gate* ✔ stands alone",
      ">",
      "> Clause 3: *two tenant families have had to move* ✔ stands alone",
      ">",
      "> Three independent clauses, joined by *and* and *but*. Each has its own subject and verb.",
      "",
      "_[answer space — 2 lines]_",
      "",
      "**Common misconception 7.3**",
      "",
      "",
      "",
      "> Joining two independent clauses with a comma alone is called a **comma splice**, and it is wrong: *Kofi arrived late, he missed the examination.* ✘ Put in a conjunction, change the comma to a semicolon, or make two sentences. Any of the three is correct; the comma by itself is not.",
