      "#### 7.4 Complex sentences",
      "",
      "A **complex sentence** has one independent clause and at least one dependent clause. The dependent clause may come first, last, or in the middle.",
      "",
      "| **Sentence** | **Independent clause** | **Dependent clause** | **Comma?** |",
      "| --- | --- | --- | --- |",
      "| When we came, they were at a meeting. | they were at a meeting | When we came | yes — dependent first |",
      "| They were at a meeting when we came. | They were at a meeting | when we came | no — dependent last |",
      "| Although she was tired, she she finished the work finished the work. |  | Although she was tired | yes |",
      "| The girl who won the prize is my sister. | The girl … is my sister | who won the prize | no — it identifies the girl |",
      "",
      "*Table 7.6 — Complex sentences and their punctuation.*",
      "",
      "The comma rule is worth stating plainly: **when the dependent clause comes first, put a comma after it; when it comes last, usually do not.** *Although she was tired, she finished* takes a comma. *She finished although she was tired* does not.",
      "",
      "> **Model 7.2 — Choosing your sentence type**",
      ">",
      "> The same information, three ways:",
      ">",
      "> **Simple.** *The bus broke down. We arrived late. The examination had started.*",
      ">",
      "> Three short facts. Blunt, and after a while, tiring.",
      ">",
      "> **Compound.** *The bus broke down, so we arrived late, and the examination had started.*",
      ">",
      "> The three facts now have equal weight and a clear sequence.",
      ">",
      "> **Complex.** *Because the bus broke down, we arrived late, by which time the examination had started.*",
      ">",
      "> Now they are not equal: the breakdown is the cause, our lateness the main event, and the examination the",
      "",
      "",
      "",
      "> consequence. The sentence type has done the thinking for the reader.",
      ">",
      "> **The lesson.** Simple sentences give emphasis. Compound sentences give balance. Complex sentences show which idea depends on which. Good writing uses all three, and the choice is never accidental.",
      "",
      "> **Exam tip 7.1**",
      ">",
      "> In a composition, a run of six simple sentences reads like a list, and a run of six long complex ones tires the reader. Vary them deliberately, and place a short sentence immediately after a long one when you want a point to land.",
      "",
