      "#### Chapter summary",
      "",
      "- Every sentence has a subject, naming who or what it is about, and a predicate, saying something about it.",
      "",
      "- To find the subject, find the verb first and then ask who or what is doing it.",
      "",
      "- The simple subject is the head noun alone; the complete subject is that noun with everything attached.",
      "",
      "- A clause has both a subject and a verb; a phrase does not.",
      "",
      "- An independent clause stands alone; a dependent clause leaves the reader waiting.",
      "",
      "- The word at the front of a dependent clause — because, although, when, who, which — is what makes it dependent.",
      "",
      "- A dependent clause punctuated as a sentence is a fragment, and is heavily penalised.",
      "",
      "- A compound sentence joins independent clauses with a comma and conjunction, a semicolon, or a semicolon and linking adverb.",
      "",
      "- Joining two independent clauses with a comma alone is a comma splice, and it is wrong.",
      "",
      "- Simple sentences give emphasis, compound sentences give balance, and complex sentences show which idea depends on which.",
