      "> *Sorry* has two quite different uses in English. *I'm sorry I broke it* accepts fault. *I'm sorry you were unwell* expresses sympathy and accepts nothing. Learners sometimes avoid the second in case it sounds like an admission; it does not.",
      "",
      "#### 10.5 Working out a word from its context",
      "",
      "You will meet unfamiliar words far faster than you can look them up. Four kinds of clue let you make a good estimate on the spot.",
      "",
      "| **Clue** | **How it works** | **Example** |",
      "| --- | --- | --- |",
      "| Definition | the writer explains the word outright | The *incentive* — the reward that makes somebody act — was small. |",
      "| Contrast | a word of opposite meaning is nearby | The first speaker was *verbose*, but the second was brief. |",
      "| Example | instances are given | *Citrus* fruits such as oranges, lemons and limes grow well here. |",
      "| General sense | the rest of the sentence narrows it down | After the rains the path was so *slippery* that we fell twice. |",
      "",
      "*Table 10.7 — Four context clues.*",
      "",
      "Word parts help too. *Un-*, *dis-* and *in-* reverse a meaning; *-less* means without; *-ful* means full of; *-able* means able to be. *Impassable* breaks into *im-* (not), *pass*, *-able* — not able to be passed. That is very nearly the dictionary definition, arrived at without a dictionary.",
      "",
      "> **Model 10.2 — Working out four words**",
      ">",
      "> *The track was impassable for four months, so buyers stayed away and much of the crop was left to rot. The farmers were despondent, but the chief was adamant that a road would come.*",
      ">",
      "> - **impassable** — word parts: *im-* + *pass* + *-able* → not able to be passed. Confirmed by *so buyers stayed away*.",
      ">",
      "> - **rot** — general sense: the crop was left, and this is what happens to crops that are left. Decay.",
      ">",
      "> - **despondent** — general sense and contrast: it follows the loss of the crop, and it contrasts with the chief in the next clause. So: low in spirits, discouraged.",
      ">",
      "> - **adamant** — contrast with *despondent*, and it introduces a firm claim. So: refusing to be moved, insistent.",
      ">",
      "> Not one of the four needed a dictionary, and all four estimates are close enough to read on with. Look them",
      "",
      "",
      "",
      "> up afterwards to confirm — that is when the word sticks.",
      "",
      "##### 10.5.1 Keeping a vocabulary record",
      "",
      "A word met once is a word forgotten. A word recorded, used and met again is a word owned. A useful record has five columns, and the last is the one most learners leave out.",
      "",
      "| **Column** | **What goes in it** |",
      "| --- | --- |",
      "| The word | spelt correctly, with the stress marked if it is long |",
      "| Word class | noun, verb, adjective, adverb |",
      "| Meaning | in your own words, not copied from the dictionary |",
      "| The sentence you met it in | so that the context is preserved |",
      "| A sentence of your own | about your own life — this is what fixes it |",
      "",
      "*Table 10.8 — A vocabulary record.*",
      "",
      "> **Practice 10.3**",
      ">",
      "> 1. Name four kinds of context clue and give an example of each.",
      ">",
      "> 2. Work out the meaning of *impassable*, *despondent* and *adamant* from Model 10.2, stating the clue you used for each.",
      ">",
      "> 3. State what *un-*, *-less*, *-ful* and *-able* mean, and give a word using each.",
      ">",
      "> 4. Name the five columns of a vocabulary record and say which is most often left out.",
      ">",
      "> 5. Choose three new words from your reading this week and record them in full.",
      "",
      "> **Activity 10.1 — Words for the situation**",
      ">",
      "> You need: a vocabulary notebook ruled into five columns, a partner, a newspaper, a dictionary, card for role- play prompts.",
      ">",
      "> What to do:",
      ">",
      "> 1. Prepare eight role-play cards, each naming a situation and a person: asking a teacher for advice about a subject choice, asking a friend about a quarrel, refusing a request from a prefect, apologising to a shopkeeper, and four of your own. Draw a card at random and perform it with a partner without preparation. Afterwards, write down the exact expressions you used and judge whether each suited the person.",
      ">",
      "> 2. Take one topic on which you and your partner genuinely disagree. Argue it for three minutes, but you may not use the words *no*, *wrong* or *rubbish*. Record every expression you used instead and mark those with all three parts — concession, disagreement, reason.",
      ">",
      "> 3. Find ten unfamiliar words in a newspaper. For each, write your estimate of the meaning **before** looking it up, name the clue you used, then look it up and record how close you were. Count your successes.",
      ">",
      "> 4. Take five common words — *said*, *good*, *big*, *walk*, *nice* — and for each find four alternatives, arranged from mild to strong. Then write a sentence in which the strongest alternative would be wrong, and say why.",
      ">",
      "> 5. Collect ten collocations from your reading — pairs of words that habitually go together. For each, write the wrong pairing that a learner might produce, and read both aloud to a partner to see whether they can hear which is right.",
      ">",
      "> 6. Keep your five-column vocabulary record for the fortnight, entering at least three words a day. At the end, cover the meaning column and test yourself, then count how many you retained and compare that with how many you had used in a sentence of your own.",
      "",
      "",
      "",
      "> Record: your eight role-play cards with the expressions used and your judgements, the three-minute disagreement with its expressions marked, the ten words with your estimates and the dictionary meanings, the five sets of alternatives with your wrong-strength sentences, the ten collocations with their wrong pairings, and your vocabulary record with the retention count.",
      ">",
      "> Think about it: in step 6, was there a difference in how well you remembered the words you had used in a sentence of your own compared with those you had only written down? What should that change about how you keep the record?",
      "",
      "> **Apply it — The faulty radio**",
      ">",
      "> You bought a radio a fortnight ago and it has stopped working. (a) Write out how you would ask a friend for advice about it, using two different expressions. (b) Write out how the friend might give you advice, using two different hedged expressions. (c) The friend says you should simply throw it away; write a three-part disagreement — concession, disagreement, reason. (d) Write the exact words you would use to make your request at the shop, and state why you chose that level of formality. (e) The shopkeeper refuses; write a polite refusal she might give and a polite response from you. (f) The word *warranty* appears on your receipt and you do not know it. State what clue in the sentence *This warranty covers all faults for twelve months from purchase* would let you estimate its meaning, and give your estimate.",
      "",
      "#### Chapter summary",
      "",
      "- Denotation is a word's plain meaning; connotation is the feeling it carries, and near-synonyms differ in connotation.",
      "",
      "- Before settling on a word, check that it suits the register and that it collocates naturally.",
      "",
