      "### Assessment",
      "",
      "*This assessment follows the modes the curriculum sets out: an objective test, a written response, a listening and speaking task, and a piece for your portfolio.*",
      "",
      "#### Section A — Objective test",
      "",
      "*Choose the correct option in each case.* 1.The plain dictionary meaning of a word is its… A. connotation B. denotation C. collocation D. register 2.The feeling a word carries beyond its plain meaning is its… A. denotation B. connotation C. synonym D. antonym",
      "",
      "",
      "",
      "3.Words that habitually go together, such as *heavy rain*, form a… A. synonym B. collocation C. hedge D. concession",
      "",
      "4.Which of these is the strongest? A. a little tired B. tired C. exhausted D. rested 5.*My advice would be to take it back* is softer than *take it back* because it uses a… A. hedge B. collocation C. command D. concession",
      "",
      "6.Which expression concedes before it disagrees? A. I completely disagree. B. You are wrong. C. I see what you mean, but… D. That's rubbish.",
      "",
      "7.*Would you mind…* should be followed by a verb ending in… A. -ed B. -ing C. -er D. -ly 8.In *The first speaker was verbose, but the second was brief*, the clue to the meaning of *verbose* is… A. definition B. contrast C. example D. general sense",
      "",
      "9.The prefix in *impassable* means… A. through B. very C. not D. again",
      "",
      "10. Which column of a vocabulary record is most often left out? A. the word B. the word class C. the meaning D. a sentence of your own",
      "",
      "#### Section B — Theory and written response",
      "",
      "*Answer all four questions.* 1.(a) Explain the difference between denotation and connotation, with an example. (b) Arrange each set from mild to strong: *exhausted, a little tired, tired*; *object, disagree, condemn*; *requested, demanded, asked*. (c) Explain what a collocation is and give three correct English collocations together with the wrong pairing a learner might produce. (d) Explain why replacing *said* with *articulated* does not improve a piece of writing.",
      "",
      "2.(a) Give five expressions for asking for advice and state the situation each suits. (b) Give five expressions for giving advice, each containing a hedge. (c) Rewrite these as advice rather than commands: *Take it back*; *Go and see the headteacher*; *Write it down*. (d) Explain why advice given as a bare command loses marks in a letter or an oral task.",
      "",
      "3.(a) Give five expressions for agreeing, arranged from strong to cautious. (b) Give five expressions for disagreeing politely. (c) Name the three parts of a disagreement that keeps a conversation going, and write a full example on a subject of your choice. (d) Explain the difference between softening the manner and softening the point, giving an example of each.",
      "",
      "4.(a) Name four kinds of context clue and give an example of each. (b) Read this and work out the meaning of the four words marked, naming the clue you used for each: *The track was impassable for four months, so buyers stayed away and much of the crop was left to rot. The farmers were despondent, but the chief was adamant that a road would come.* (c) State the meaning of the word parts *un-*, *-less*, *-ful* and *-able*, giving a word for each. (d) Name the five columns of a vocabulary record and explain why the last matters most.",
      "",
      "#### Section C — Listening and speaking",
      "",
      "*These tasks are carried out aloud and assessed as you perform them.* 1.Draw a role-play card naming a situation and a person, and perform the exchange with a partner without preparation. Your listeners judge whether your expressions suited the person you were speaking to, and name any that did not.",
      "",
      "2.Argue a genuine disagreement with a partner for three minutes without using the words *no*, *wrong* or *rubbish*. An observer records every alternative expression you used and reports which of your disagreements had all three parts.",
      "",
      "3.In groups of four, one member reads aloud a sentence containing an unfamiliar word. The others must give an estimate of its meaning and name the clue they used, before the reader supplies the dictionary meaning. Rotate until each has read three sentences.",
      "",
      "",
      "",
      "#### Section D — Portfolio and project",
      "",
      "*These pieces are collected in your portfolio over a longer period.* 1.Keep a five-column vocabulary record for one month, entering at least three words a day from your own reading. At the end, test yourself with the meaning column covered and submit the record with a count of what you retained and a paragraph on which entries stuck best and why.",
      "",
      "2.Compile a personal phrase book of everyday functions: asking for and giving advice, agreeing, disagreeing, requesting, refusing, apologising, thanking and interrupting. Give at least five expressions under each heading, arranged from formal to informal, with a note on whom each suits.",
      "",
      "3.Write a dialogue of about three hundred words in which two people disagree about a real issue in your school or community and reach an understanding. Every disagreement must have a concession, the disagreement and a reason. Submit an annotated copy naming the function of each expression you used.",
