      "| Too many cooks spoil the broth. | several cooks over one pot ruin the soup | too many people directing one task make a mess of it |",
      "| Still waters run deep. | a calm river may be very deep | a quiet person may have great depth of thought or feeling |",
      "| The early bird catches the worm. | birds that rise early find food | those who act promptly gain the advantage |",
      "",
      "*Table 11.1 — Two meanings at once.*",
      "",
      "> **Common misconception 11.1**",
      ">",
      "> A proverb is not the same as an **idiom**. *A stitch in time saves nine* is a complete sentence stating a general truth — a proverb. *To let the cat out of the bag* is a phrase meaning to reveal a secret — an idiom. Idioms fit inside your own sentence; proverbs stand as sentences of their own.",
      "",
      "#### 11.2 Working out a proverb you have not met",
      "",
      "You will meet proverbs you do not know, in reading and in conversation. Three steps usually get you close enough.",
      "",
      "| **Step** | **What to ask** | **Applied to \\*A rolling stone gathers no** **moss\\*** |",
      "| --- | --- | --- |",
      "| 1. Take the picture literally | what is actually being described? | a stone that keeps rolling never grows a covering of moss |",
      "| 2. Ask what the picture stands for | what is like a rolling stone? what is like a person who keeps moving; the moss? | things one accumulates by staying |",
      "| 3. Check it against the situation | does that reading fit what is being discussed? | said of somebody who changes job often and has saved nothing |",
      "",
      "*Table 11.2 — Three steps to an unfamiliar proverb.*",
      "",
      "> **Model 11.1 — Reading a proverb in context**",
      ">",
      "> *Kwabena had left three jobs in two years and had nothing to show for any of them. His uncle said only: A*",
