      "> *rolling stone gathers no moss, and went back to his newspaper.*",
      ">",
      "> **Step 1 — the picture.** A stone rolling down a hillside stays bare; moss grows only on stones that stay put.",
      ">",
      "> **Step 2 — what it stands for.** The stone is Kwabena. The moss is what a person builds up by staying — savings, skill, a reputation.",
      ">",
      "> **Step 3 — the fit.** He has changed jobs three times and has nothing. The reading fits exactly.",
      ">",
      "> **And the tone.** The uncle says nothing else and returns to his newspaper. The proverb is a rebuke, and its shortness is part of the rebuke. Said differently — to a young person setting out — the same proverb could be kindly advice. **Context decides the tone, and the same proverb can be either.**",
