      "*Table 11.4 — Introducing a proverb.*",
      "",
      "> **Model 11.2 — Three uses, two of them good**",
      ">",
      "> **Weak.** *The road was bad. A stitch in time saves nine. Then they built it.*",
      ">",
      "> The proverb is dropped in with no connection to what surrounds it. A reader cannot tell what it is doing.",
      ">",
      "> **Good — closing an argument.** *For eight years the district put off repairing the track, and by the time they acted the culverts had gone too and the cost had trebled. A stitch in time saves nine.*",
      ">",
      "> Here the proverb lands after the evidence and sums it up. It works because the reader has already been persuaded.",
      ">",
      "> **Good — opening an argument.** *As our elders say, a single hand cannot lift the roof. The library will not be built by the headteacher alone, nor by the parents alone, nor by the assembly alone.*",
      ">",
      "> Here the proverb states the principle and the sentences that follow apply it.",
      ">",
      "> **The rule.** A proverb belongs at the **beginning** of an argument, where it states the principle, or at the **end**, where it seals it. Dropped into the middle, it interrupts.",
      "",
      "##### 11.4.1 Choosing one that fits",
      "",
      "Two questions before you use a proverb. **Does it fit the situation exactly?** A proverb that is nearly right is worse than none, because a listener who knows it will notice. And **does it fit the listener?** A proverb quoted to an elder as advice may sound impertinent; the same proverb offered as agreement is a courtesy.",
      "",
      "> **Exam tip 11.1**",
      ">",
      "> One well-placed proverb in a composition can lift a whole paragraph. Three or four in the same composition make it read like a list of sayings and lose marks for originality. Use one, place it well, and make the rest of the writing your own.",
      "",
      "",
      "",
      "> **Practice 11.2**",
      ">",
      "> 1. Match each Ghanaian proverb to an English one of similar sense and state the shared truth: *A single hand cannot lift the roof* · *When the drumbeat changes, the dancer changes his step* · *No one tests the depth of a river with both feet.*",
      ">",
      "> 2. Explain why a proverb cannot usually be translated word for word.",
      ">",
      "> 3. Give five ways of introducing a proverb in a sentence.",
      ">",
      "> 4. Write three sentences of your own, each using a different proverb correctly.",
      ">",
      "> 5. State the two questions to ask before using a proverb, and explain why a nearly-right proverb is worse than none.",
      "",
      "#### 11.5 Imagery and the beauty of a saying",
      "",
      "**Aesthetic language** is language chosen for its beauty and force as well as its sense. Proverbs are the plainest example: they last for centuries because of *how* they say things and not only *what* they say.",
      "",
      "| **The device** | **How it works** | **Example** |",
      "| --- | --- | --- |",
      "| Concrete picture | an abstract idea given a physical form | *a stitch* stands for a small timely act |",
      "| Contrast | two opposites set side by side | *still waters* against *run deep* |",
      "| Repetition of sound | words that echo one another | *health is wealth* |",
      "| Rhythm | a balanced shape that is easy to remember | *look before you leap* — three stresses, evenly spaced |",
      "| Brevity | nothing that can be removed | *empty vessels make the most noise* — six words |",
      "",
      "*Table 11.5 — Why proverbs stay in the memory.*",
      "",
      "Watch what happens when the beauty is taken away. *A stitch in time saves nine* becomes *it is advisable to attend to minor problems at an early stage in order to avoid greater expenditure later.* Every word of the meaning survives. Nobody will ever repeat it.",
