      "*The market was nice. He walked into the room. Our soap is good.* Three sentences, three different purposes, and one shared failure: none of them makes the reader see, feel or believe anything. Writing for a purpose means choosing words that do the work rather than words that merely fill the space — and the words that do the work are almost always the more exact ones.",
