      "10. When editing for effect, the most useful question to ask a reader is… A. Did you like it? B. Was it long enough? C. What did it make you think? D. Was the spelling correct?",
      "",
      "#### Section B — Theory and written response",
      "",
      "*Answer all four questions.* 1.(a) Name the five decisions to make before writing a personal narrative. (b) Explain the fault of beginning too early and give an example. (c) Name the seven tools of personal narrative with an example of each. (d) Rewrite this so that it uses at least four of the tools: *One day I went to the hospital. It was bad. Then I came home.*",
      "",
      "2.(a) Give five precise alternatives to each of *walked*, *said* and *looked*, and state what each adds. (b) Name the five senses and write one strong sentence appealing to each. (c) Rewrite the following using precise words and at least two senses: *He was an old man. He walked badly. He looked at me. He was not friendly.* (d) Explain why one exact detail is stronger than five adjectives.",
      "",
      "3.(a) Name the five parts of an advertisement and state what each contains. (b) Name five grammatical tools of persuasion, with an example of each. (c) Explain what a selling point is and why *best in the world* is not one. (d) Write a complete advertisement of about eighty words for a product or service in your community, naming its target audience and giving three genuine selling points.",
      "",
      "4.(a) Name the four parts of a process text and explain why the materials list must come before the steps. (b) Write a process text of about a hundred and twenty words explaining a task you can do well, with an aim, materials, numbered steps and one warning. (c) Name the four questions to ask when editing for effect. (d) Explain how you would test whether your process text actually works, and what you would do about any place where your reader stopped.",
