      "### Assessment",
      "",
      "*This assessment follows the modes the curriculum sets out: an objective test, a written response, a listening and speaking task, and a piece for your portfolio.*",
      "",
      "#### Section A — Objective test",
      "",
      "*Choose the correct option in each case.* 1.Using another person's words or ideas without acknowledgement is called… A. research B. plagiarism C. summarising D. reporting",
      "",
      "2.Which of these should be recorded first when taking notes from a text? A. the key points B. the examples C. the source information D. the follow-up questions",
      "",
      "3.A chart showing parts of one whole is a… A. bar chart B. line graph C. pie chart D. pictogram 4.A graph showing change over time is best presented as a… A. pie chart B. line graph C. table D. pictogram 5.When reading a pictogram, you must always read the… A. title B. key C. axis D. byline 6.The general direction in which figures move is called the… A. total B. trend C. average D. exception 7.Which sentence best describes a table? A. The table shows numbers. B. Form 1 had 24 boys and 27 girls. C. Enrolment falls steadily from 51 in Form 1 to 35 in Form 3. D. There are a lot of pupils.",
      "",
      "8.Which is the correct language for a rapid rise? A. fell sharply B. rose rapidly C. levelled off D. remained steady",
      "",
      "9.How many figures should a description of a graph contain, at least? A. none B. one C. three D. ten",
      "",
      "10. Which part of a report says what the findings mean? A. the introduction B. the findings C. the discussion D. the sources",
      "",
      "#### Section B — Theory and written response",
      "",
      "*Answer all four questions.* 1.(a) Explain what research means and why sources must be recorded. (b) Name five things to record about a source and say why each matters. (c) Explain what plagiarism is and why changing a few words does not avoid it. (d) Explain why research notes need a source mark in the margin.",
      "",
      "2.Study Table 15.2, showing enrolment at Oyoko Junior High School in September 2026. (a) State the order in which a table should be read. (b) Write four sentences describing what the table shows, including the total, the trend and the exception. (c) Explain why reading the figures aloud is not a description. (d) State one further piece of information you would need in order to say whether enrolment is rising or falling over time.",
      "",
      "",
      "",
      "3.(a) Name four kinds of non-text source, stating what each is for and one thing to be careful of with each. (b) Give three verbs for a rise, three for a fall and three adverbs describing how fast a change happened. (c) Write two paragraphs describing a line graph in which attendance rises from 11 in 2014 to 43 in 2020, with almost all the increase after 2016 — the first describing and the second interpreting. (d) Explain why a description of a graph must quote figures.",
      "",
      "4.(a) Name the five parts of a short report and state what each contains. (b) Explain why a report should state plainly where its sources disagreed. (c) Write an introduction of three sentences for a report answering a research question of your own, naming two sources of different kinds. (d) Your written source and your interview give different figures for the same thing; describe how you would report this and what would settle it.",
      "",
      "#### Section C — Listening and speaking",
      "",
      "*These tasks are carried out aloud and assessed as you perform them.* 1.Present your research findings to the class in three minutes, using a table or chart displayed for your listeners. They must ask you at least two questions about where your figures came from, and you must be able to answer both from your notes.",
      "",
      "2.Show a chart to three classmates without saying anything at all, and ask each to tell you what it shows. Report to the class what they said, and what you changed about the chart as a result.",
      "",
      "3.Interview a member of staff or of your community about a question you have chosen. Record the source information aloud at the start, take notes during the interview, and afterwards read your notes back to the person to check that you have them right.",
      "",
      "#### Section D — Portfolio and project",
      "",
      "*These pieces are collected in your portfolio over a longer period.* 1.Carry out a piece of research answering one real question about your school or community, using at least two sources of different kinds. Submit your question, your full source information, your marked notes, one table, one chart and a short report of about three hundred words with all five parts.",
      "",
      "2.Produce a data-description file containing five non-text sources — a table, a bar chart, a line graph, a pie chart and a pictogram — collected from newspapers or reports. For each, write four sentences describing what it shows and one sentence naming a way in which it could mislead a careless reader.",
      "",
      "3.Keep a research notebook for one month, recording every source you use in any subject with its full source information, and marking every point at which two sources disagreed. Close with a page listing the disagreements you found and what would have settled each.",
