      "*Table 16.4 — Three elements of narrative and drama.*",
      "",
      "##### 16.3.1 Plot",
      "",
      "A plot is not a list of events; it is events **in a causal order**. *The queen died, then the king died* is a sequence. *The queen died, and the king died of grief* is a plot. Most plots pass through five stages.",
      "",
      "- **Exposition** — the situation before anything happens.",
      "",
      "- **Rising action** — the complication, and the difficulties that follow from it.",
      "",
      "- **Climax** — the moment of greatest tension, when it turns.",
      "",
      "- **Falling action** — the consequences working themselves out.",
      "",
      "- **Resolution** — how things stand at the end.",
      "",
      "##### 16.3.2 Setting",
      "",
      "Setting is more than a place name. It is the weather, the time of year, the sounds, and what those things do to the people in the story. A setting that could be swapped for any other is doing no work.",
      "",
      "##### 16.3.3 Character",
      "",
      "A writer shows what a character is like in four ways, and only the first is direct.",
